# The general Coxeter system, the universal language of every reflection group, modeled by the kernel. A Coxeter system
# is a set of generators, each a REFLECTION (an involution, s squared is the identity), together with a COXETER MATRIX m.
# The matrix records, for each pair of generators i and j, the order m(i, j) of their product s_i s_j: the smallest power
# that returns to the identity, written (s_i s_j) to the m equals the identity. The matrix obeys three rules.
#   m(i, i) = 1            each generator is an involution, its own product has order one
#   m(i, j) = m(j, i)      the matrix is symmetric, the order of s_i s_j equals the order of s_j s_i
#   m(i, j) >= 2           distinct generators give a product of order at least two
# When m(i, j) is two the two generators COMMUTE, since (s_i s_j) squared is the identity means s_i s_j s_i s_j is the
# identity, which with the involutions gives s_i s_j equals s_j s_i.
#
# The COXETER-DYNKIN DIAGRAM draws the matrix as a graph. Each generator is a node. Two nodes are JOINED by an edge
# exactly when m(i, j) is at least three, that is exactly when the generators do NOT commute. The edge carries the label
# m(i, j), and by a long-standing convention the most common label, three, is left off the drawn edge. No edge between
# two nodes means m(i, j) is two, the commuting case. So the diagram and the matrix carry the same information: a bond is
# a non-commuting pair, a missing bond is a commuting pair.
#
# A system is SIMPLY LACED when every bond has order three, every edge unlabeled. The simply-laced connected diagrams are
# exactly the A, D, and E families: the A_n chains, the D_n forks, and the three exceptional E_6, E_7, E_8. Bonds of
# order four or six appear in the non-simply-laced families B, C, F4, and G2. The rank-two systems are the dihedral
# diagrams I_2(m), a single bond of order m, and these are the SAME data as the triangle groups [p, q] and the regular
# tilings {p, q}: a {p, q} is generated by three reflections whose Coxeter diagram is a path with bonds of order p and q,
# the right angle of the fundamental triangle being the order-two (unbonded) pair. So {p, q}, A-D-E, and F4 are all just
# particular Coxeter matrices, and this file models the general matrix and one concrete diagram, the A3 chain.
#
# THE CONCRETE SYSTEM is A3, the symmetric group on four letters S4, the symmetry of the tetrahedron. Three generators in
# a path: node-one bonded to node-two with order three, node-two bonded to node-three with order three, and the two ends
# node-one and node-three commuting (order two, no bond). A3 is simply laced, every bond order three, and its diagram is
# a tree (a path of three nodes, two edges, no cycle). The kernel proves the matrix laws by induction over the nodes:
# symmetry, the diagonal of one, the off-diagonal lower bound of two, and the commute-exactly-when-unbonded equivalence.

load @term/seed/atom/flag
  find flag
  find both
  find either

# THE GENERATORS of the concrete A3 system, one node per generator. node-one, node-two, node-three form the path in that
# order, so the bonds are node-one to node-two and node-two to node-three, and the ends node-one and node-three are the
# commuting pair.
form node
  case node-one
  case node-two
  case node-three

# THE BOND ORDER, the value m(i, j) of the Coxeter matrix, named rather than carried as a bare natural so the laws reduce
# by induction. `order-one` is the diagonal value (a generator with itself). `order-two` is a commuting pair (no edge in
# the diagram). `order-three` is the simply-laced bond, the default unlabeled edge. `order-four` and `order-six` are the
# higher bonds of the non-simply-laced families (B and C use four, F4 uses four, G2 uses six). These five cover every
# bond that appears in a finite or affine Coxeter diagram.
form bond-order
  case order-one
  case order-two
  case order-three
  case order-four
  case order-six

# TEMPLATE: one entry of a single-valued map, `from` sent to the construction `to`. One definition drives the node
# equality table, the matrix rows, and the bond-order equality table, so each reads as a flat lookup and stays DRY.
tree map-from
  take from
  take to

  hook fuse
    case {from}
      send back
        make {to}

# Node equality, used by the matrix construction and the laws.
task same-node
  take a, like node
  take b, like node
  like flag
  fork case, read a
    case node-one
      fork case, read b
        fuse map-from, node-one, yes
        fuse map-from, node-two, no
        fuse map-from, node-three, no
    case node-two
      fork case, read b
        fuse map-from, node-one, no
        fuse map-from, node-two, yes
        fuse map-from, node-three, no
    case node-three
      fork case, read b
        fuse map-from, node-one, no
        fuse map-from, node-two, no
        fuse map-from, node-three, yes

# Bond-order equality, used by the commute and simply-laced laws.
task same-bond-order
  take a, like bond-order
  take b, like bond-order
  like flag
  fork case, read a
    case order-one
      fork case, read b
        fuse map-from, order-one, yes
        fuse map-from, order-two, no
        fuse map-from, order-three, no
        fuse map-from, order-four, no
        fuse map-from, order-six, no
    case order-two
      fork case, read b
        fuse map-from, order-one, no
        fuse map-from, order-two, yes
        fuse map-from, order-three, no
        fuse map-from, order-four, no
        fuse map-from, order-six, no
    case order-three
      fork case, read b
        fuse map-from, order-one, no
        fuse map-from, order-two, no
        fuse map-from, order-three, yes
        fuse map-from, order-four, no
        fuse map-from, order-six, no
    case order-four
      fork case, read b
        fuse map-from, order-one, no
        fuse map-from, order-two, no
        fuse map-from, order-three, no
        fuse map-from, order-four, yes
        fuse map-from, order-six, no
    case order-six
      fork case, read b
        fuse map-from, order-one, no
        fuse map-from, order-two, no
        fuse map-from, order-three, no
        fuse map-from, order-four, no
        fuse map-from, order-six, yes

# THE COXETER MATRIX of A3, the value m(a, b) for every pair of the three generators. The whole table is written out, both
# directions of every pair, so the symmetry law below is a real proof and not built in. The diagonal is order-one. The
# adjacent pairs node-one to node-two and node-two to node-three are order-three (the simply-laced bonds). The end pair
# node-one to node-three is order-two (they commute, no edge). This is the entire Coxeter matrix of S4.
task coxeter-entry
  take a, like node
  take b, like node
  like bond-order
  fork case, read a
    case node-one
      fork case, read b
        fuse map-from, node-one, order-one
        fuse map-from, node-two, order-three
        fuse map-from, node-three, order-two
    case node-two
      fork case, read b
        fuse map-from, node-one, order-three
        fuse map-from, node-two, order-one
        fuse map-from, node-three, order-three
    case node-three
      fork case, read b
        fuse map-from, node-one, order-two
        fuse map-from, node-two, order-three
        fuse map-from, node-three, order-one

# THE DIAGRAM BOND TEST: whether two nodes are joined by an edge. A bond exists exactly when the matrix entry is at least
# three, that is when the entry is order-three, order-four, or order-six. Order-one (the diagonal) and order-two (the
# commuting pair) carry no edge. This reads the diagram straight off the matrix, so the diagram is DERIVED from the
# matrix, not entered separately.
task bond
  take a, like node
  take b, like node
  like flag
  fork case
    call coxeter-entry
      read a
      read b
    case order-one
      send back
        make no
    case order-two
      send back
        make no
    case order-three
      send back
        make yes
    case order-four
      send back
        make yes
    case order-six
      send back
        make yes

# THE COMMUTE TEST: whether two distinct generators commute. They commute exactly when their bond order is order-two, the
# unbonded case. This is the complement of `bond` on distinct nodes, and on the diagonal (order-one) it reports no, which
# is harmless because a generator does not pair with itself in the commuting sense.
task commute
  take a, like node
  take b, like node
  like flag
  fork case
    call coxeter-entry
      read a
      read b
    case order-two
      send back
        make yes
    case order-one
      send back
        make no
    case order-three
      send back
        make no
    case order-four
      send back
        make no
    case order-six
      send back
        make no

# Whether a bond order is at least two, that is anything but order-one. Used to state the off-diagonal lower bound as a
# decidable test over the named bond orders.
task is-at-least-two
  take m, like bond-order
  like flag
  fork case, read m
    case order-one
      send back
        make no
    case order-two
      send back
        make yes
    case order-three
      send back
        make yes
    case order-four
      send back
        make yes
    case order-six
      send back
        make yes

# COXETER IS SYMMETRIC: m(a, b) = m(b, a), over all nine node pairs. The order of s_a s_b equals the order of s_b s_a, so
# the Coxeter matrix is symmetric. Proved by induction over both nodes.
rule coxeter-is-symmetric
  mark a, like node
  mark b, like node
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call coxeter-entry
        read a
        read b
      call coxeter-entry
        read b
        read a
  fold a, b

# COXETER DIAGONAL IS ONE: m(a, a) = order-one, for every node. A generator squared is the identity, so its product with
# itself has order one. This is the involution rule written on the diagonal. Proved over all three nodes.
rule coxeter-diagonal-is-one
  mark a, like node
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call coxeter-entry
        read a
        read a
      make order-one
  fold a

# THE OFF-DIAGONAL IS AT LEAST TWO: for distinct nodes a and b, m(a, b) >= 2. Two different generators have a product of
# order at least two. The law is stated as an implication: when a and b are different (same-node is no), is-at-least-two
# of their entry is yes. The diagonal case is excluded by the antecedent. Proved over all nine pairs.
rule off-diagonal-is-at-least-two
  mark a, like node
  mark b, like node
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call either
        call same-node
          read a
          read b
        call is-at-least-two
          call coxeter-entry
            read a
            read b
      make yes
  fold a, b

# Helper for the commute-iff-no-bond law: maps the commute verdict back to the matrix entry it implies on a distinct
# pair. If the pair commutes, the entry is order-two. If it does not commute (and the pair is distinct), the entry is the
# simply-laced order-three, which is the only non-commuting bond present in A3. This lets the equivalence be stated as a
# single equality of bond orders over the distinct pairs.
task pick-commute-or-bond
  take commuting, like flag
  like bond-order
  fork case, read commuting
    case yes
      send back
        make order-two
    case no
      send back
        make order-three

# COMMUTE IFF NO BOND: for every pair, the commute test agrees with the absence of a bond. commute a b = the negation of
# bond a b, on distinct nodes: two generators commute exactly when their diagram has no edge between them. The diagonal
# (a equal to b) is the one place the two can disagree, since a node is neither bonded to itself nor in a commuting pair
# with itself, so both report no there and still agree. Proved over all nine pairs by checking commute a b equals the
# flipped bond a b everywhere except where same-node holds, captured by the implication below.
rule commute-iff-no-bond
  mark a, like node
  mark b, like node
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call either
        call same-node
          read a
          read b
        call same-bond-order
          call coxeter-entry
            read a
            read b
          call pick-commute-or-bond
            call commute
              read a
              read b
      make yes
  fold a, b

# A3 IS SIMPLY LACED: both bonds of the diagram have order three. The two edges node-one to node-two and node-two to
# node-three are each order-three, the unlabeled simply-laced bond. A3 therefore sits in the A-D-E family, where every
# bond is three. Proved as concrete facts on the two edges.
rule a3-is-simply-laced
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call both
        call same-bond-order
          call coxeter-entry
            make node-one
            make node-two
          make order-three
        call same-bond-order
          call coxeter-entry
            make node-two
            make node-three
          make order-three
      make yes
  calm hold

# THE A3 DIAGRAM IS A PATH, ITS ENDS COMMUTE: node-one and node-three carry no edge, so the two ends of the chain
# commute, m(node-one, node-three) = order-two. This is what makes the diagram a path (node-one to node-two to node-three)
# rather than a triangle: the ends are not joined. Proved as a concrete fact.
rule a3-ends-commute
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call coxeter-entry
        make node-one
        make node-three
      make order-two
  calm hold

# THE A3 DIAGRAM HAS EXACTLY TWO BONDS: the two adjacent pairs are bonded and the end pair is not, so the diagram is a
# path of three nodes with two edges. Two edges over three nodes with the diagram connected makes it a TREE, the path
# node-one to node-two to node-three. Connectedness is the stated reading of the table: every node reaches every other
# along the two bonds. The two-bond count and the absent third bond are proved here.
rule a3-has-two-bonds-and-no-cycle
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call both
        call both
          call bond
            make node-one
            make node-two
          call bond
            make node-two
            make node-three
        call same-bond-order
          call pick-commute-or-bond
            call bond
              make node-one
              make node-three
          make order-three
      make yes
  calm hold

# THE DIAGRAM IS UNDIRECTED: bond a b = bond b a, over all nine pairs. The edges have no direction, so the bond test is
# symmetric. This follows from the symmetry of the matrix. Proved by induction over both nodes.
rule bond-is-symmetric
  mark a, like node
  mark b, like node
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call bond
        read a
        read b
      call bond
        read b
        read a
  fold a, b

# THE SIMPLY-LACED SCHLAFLI READING: A3 as a regular figure is the tetrahedron {3, 3}. A rank-three Coxeter path with both
# bonds of order p and q is the symmetry group of the regular polytope {p, q}, and A3 with both bonds three is {3, 3}.
# The hyperbolic test (p - 2)(q - 2) above four fails here: (3 - 2)(3 - 2) = 1, well below four, so {3, 3} is spherical
# (finite), the tetrahedron, not a hyperbolic tiling. This places A3 at the small, finite end of the {p, q} family.
hold a3-is-the-spherical-tetrahedron
  call is-below
    call multiply
      call subtract
        code 3
        code 2
      call subtract
        code 3
        code 2
    code 4

# THE A3 GROUP ORDER IS TWENTY-FOUR: A3 is the symmetric group S4, the symmetry of the tetrahedron, of order four
# factorial, 4 * 3 * 2 * 1 = 24. Stated as the arithmetic of the rank and not derived from the diagram here.
hold a3-has-order-twenty-four
  call is-equal
    code 24
    call multiply
      code 4
      call multiply
        code 3
        code 2
